The final content_shell link fails with:

    ld: obj/content/browser/browser/browser_jumbo_99.o: in function
      `content::FontEnumerationCache::GetInstance()':
    multiple definition of `content::FontEnumerationCache::GetInstance()';
      obj/content/browser/browser/browser_jumbo_34.o: first defined here

The same "GN treats Haiku as is_linux, C++ does not" split as the undefined
symbols, but landing the other way round -- as a duplicate rather than a gap.

content/browser/BUILD.gn builds font_access/font_enumeration_cache_fontconfig.cc
for Haiku (an earlier patch added is_haiku to that condition), and that file
defines GetInstance(). The shared font_enumeration_cache.cc defines it too, but
only as a fallback:

    #if !defined(PLATFORM_HAS_LOCAL_FONT_ENUMERATION_IMPL)

and the macro is set in font_enumeration_cache.h for OS_WIN / OS_LINUX /
OS_CHROMEOS / OS_MAC. Haiku is none of those, so the fallback compiled as well
and both definitions reached the link.

Haiku genuinely does have a local implementation here -- the fontconfig one is
being built and used -- so declaring that is the correct fix rather than
dropping the fontconfig source. This also keeps font_access_manager_impl.cc
consistent, which branches on the same macro in two places to decide whether
local font enumeration is available at all.

--- a/chromium/content/browser/font_access/font_enumeration_cache.h
+++ b/chromium/content/browser/font_access/font_enumeration_cache.h
@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@
 #include "third_party/blink/public/mojom/font_access/font_access.mojom.h"
 
 #if defined(OS_WIN) || defined(OS_LINUX) || defined(OS_CHROMEOS) || \
-    defined(OS_MAC)
+    defined(OS_MAC) || defined(OS_HAIKU)
 #define PLATFORM_HAS_LOCAL_FONT_ENUMERATION_IMPL 1
 #endif
 
